<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ashkelon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ascalon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ashkelon is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of southern Israel and one of the oldest seaports of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. A Philistine city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, it later flourished under Hellenistic, Roman and Islamic rule. As Ascalon it was the last Fatimid stronghold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> until King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-iii-of-jerusalem">Baldwin III of Jerusalem</a> captured it in 1153; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> dismantled it in 1191, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> razed it in 1270.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashkelon.jpg" alt="Ashkelon" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: דוידי ורדי · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:93439_marina_ashkelon_PikiWiki_Israel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name Ashkelon is probably Western Semitic and may be connected with the root š-q-l, &quot;to weigh&quot;, perhaps pointing to the importance of the place as a centre of trade. It appears in the Egyptian execration texts in a form that suggests the earliest pronunciation was ʾaθqalānu. The ancient site, now called Tel Ashkelon, was the oldest and largest seaport in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>. It belonged to Philistia, the pentapolis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philistines">Philistines</a>, north of Gaza City and south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ascalon was an important city under Roman, Byzantine and early Muslim rule, and again in the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>, because it lay near the coast and between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ascalon">Battle of Ascalon</a> was the last action of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> ordered the fortifications and harbour destroyed in 1270, the inhabitants moved to Majdal ʿAsqalān, which he established as a substitute 3 kilometres inland, with a congregational mosque, a marketplace and religious shrines.</p><p>Ottoman records of 1596 show Majdal as a large village of 559 Muslim households, the seventh most populous place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, helped by its position on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> road. In the late Ottoman and Mandatory periods it became the main weaving centre of the Gaza District. It had about 500 looms in 1909 and about 2,000 by the 1940s, often set up in private rooms, and made striped fabrics for abaya coats and regional dresses. In 1945 its population was 9,910, of whom 9,820 were Muslims.</p><p>Egyptian forces occupied Majdal early in the 1948 war, and Israeli forces captured it in early November 1948. All but about 1,000 residents had left by then. The remaining Palestinians were confined to a fenced area and deported in stages from 17 August 1950; Israeli records give 2,333 sent to the Gaza Strip, 60 to Jordan and 302 to other towns in Israel. Jewish immigrants and demobilised soldiers settled from July 1949. The town was renamed several times, the Afridar neighbourhood for South African immigrants was incorporated in 1953, and the name Ashkelon was adopted that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The ancient site is administered as Ashkelon National Park, where the city walls, Canaanite earth ramparts and Roman, Byzantine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> ruins can be seen. The largest dog cemetery of the ancient world was found there. Majdal's Great Mosque, founded in 1300, now houses a museum. Ashkelon is the third largest city in Israel's Southern District.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ashkelon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The First Crusade took Jerusalem in 1099, and the last Levantine stronghold, <b>Ascalon</b>, fell in 1153.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Tyre fell in 1124 under his successor Baldwin II, and <b>Ascalon</b>, the last Fatimid outpost on the coast, in 1153.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A month after taking Jerusalem, the crusaders surprised and routed an Egyptian relief army near <b>Ascalon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-12">12 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After a siege begun in January, the Fatimid fortress of <b>Ascalon</b> surrendered to an army of crusaders, Templars and Hospitallers under King Baldwin III of Jerusalem.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-22">22 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It was the last Muslim seaport on the Levant but <b>Ascalon</b> to fall to the Crusaders.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-7">7 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Baldwin III captured <b>Ascalon</b> from the Fatimids in 1153, and the combined county was given to his brother Amalric.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-jaffa-and-ascalon">County of Jaffa and Ascalon</a></li></ul></section>
